Z||000011||LarryB||01-17-2001||12:11 AM||||Beowulf is layered software that lets you cluster together multiple Linux systems, for certain applications its a cheap way to throw lots of CPU horsepower at a problem.  Rack mounting is optional.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>If you want to get over 1GHz reliably....get an Athlon Thunderbird.  (Or fork over $$$ for a P-III, or get a good-quality Peltier cooler for that C566 and cross your fingers)<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||DaRkLoRD||01-17-2001||12:18 AM||||Thanks larry.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I wish I could afford this setup, I guarantee, people would be drooling over it.. especially when the front panel opens and you can see the motherboards inside, completely submerged in mineral oil....<P>(now THAT's liquid cooling. hehehehe)<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||14
Z||000013||DaRkLoRD||01-17-2001||02:53 AM||||I built a liquid cooler last year, with Tom's help in machining the coolant transfer block. I used an aluminum block with a C-shaped channel drilled into it, and threaded brass fittings siliconed in place. the coolant resevoir was a 3/4L tupperware container with 3 holes cut, for the pump's power cord and the water tubes. The aquarium pump ($40 at a pet shop) was put into the container, and it was filled with water. the water was pumped into the transfer block (which is secured directly on top of the CPU) and then it goes into a small (3.5" x 7") heater core, which I got from a rad shop for free. this heater core has two 3.5" DC fans bolted to one side. After the radiator, the cooled water flows back into the resevoir.<BR>Overall, the cooling system works very well. I got a bowl of very hot water (so hot I couldn't put by hand in it...) and held the transfer block so it was half submerged. the top of the block was COLD TO THE TOUCH.. yet the water was steaming a lot. I shut off the system, and within 2 seconds, the aluminum block was so hot it burned me.. I turned the pump back on, and it was cold again.<BR>The system doesn't leak, although the resevoir is pretty cheap.. so it can't be touched too much while it's near any power sources.. (otherwise it could spill)<BR>Although this system could be put into a computer, it's rather bulky and as I said, the resevoir is cr@p.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>I think I could build a better one if I were willing to spend the money, but I'm bored with this already.. Peltiers are great, but again, not extreme enough since the water cooler was so easy to build. (once I found the stupid radiator...)<P>I want to go with a system that's fully submerged in mineral oil (non-conductive) and cooled directly by a small refridgeration unit.. compressor and all. (using R-134 of course.. not freon)<BR>The trick will be to get the system super cold, but make it so the oil doesn't get so thick it can't circulate well enough. I'm not sure how well that will work.. I might need to keep it around freezing.. which is actually irrelevant anyway, since it'll cost hundreds of dollars to build.. money which I don't just have laying around...<P>If any of you really hardcore gamers/overclockers/whatever want to hire me to build them this system.... even as a network server with multiple servers inside the rack... I'll build it at cost, just for fun... seriously. this is something I would LOVE to do.. just hook up your own hard drives, monitor, attach it to a network hub.. pour in about 200L of mineral oil, plug it in and fire it up.. hehe.. did I mention this thing would be heavy? and probably not too quiet either.. between the compressor, oil pump and fans... it wouldn't be a good computer to keep in the bedroom. Unless you're like me, who has my computer on all night and a fan as well anyway..   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>geez that was a long post..<P>------------------<BR>   <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>   <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A><p>[This message has been edited by DaRkLoRD (edited 01-17-2001).]||||reg||6
